From e4c06280a57bcacaddad0ecd1fc98f31f5695e03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 01:32:07 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 17/22] sd53c8xx: clear the SCSI reset interrupt in busreset

busreset() pulses SCSI RST, which raises a SCSI reset interrupt
(SIST0 RST). sd53c8xxenable enables the interrupt only afterwards; the
pending interrupt then reaches sd53c8xxinterrupt late, with SMP on
another processor, after the SCRIPTS were started for the first
command and wait for a reselection. The handler sets c->running = 0
for the old reset, the next start (a write of DSP) is ignored by the
running chip, and the command times out after 600 seconds: the boot
hung in scsiverify on a QEMU lsi53c895a with 4 cpus, now and then.
busreset now reads ISTAT, SIST0, SIST1 and DSTAT after the reset,
which clears it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/src/9/pc/sd53c8xx.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sys/src/9/pc/sd53c8xx.c b/sys/src/9/pc/sd53c8xx.c
index cbb90abb3..43a4dd751 100644
--- a/sys/src/9/pc/sd53c8xx.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/pc/sd53c8xx.c
@@ -1691,6 +1691,20 @@ busreset(Controller *c)
 	c->n->scntl1 |= (1 << 3);
 	delay(500);
 	c->n->scntl1 &= ~(1 << 3);
+	/*
+	 * clear the SCSI reset interrupt the reset raised. Otherwise it
+	 * may reach the interrupt routine only after the SCRIPTS were
+	 * started for the first command (sd53c8xxenable enables the
+	 * interrupt after the reset, maybe to another processor); it sets
+	 * c->running = 0 while the SCRIPTS wait for a reselection, the
+	 * chip ignores the next start and the command times out after
+	 * 600 seconds. Reading SIST and DSTAT clears them.
+	 */
+	delay(1);
+	USED(((volatile Ncr*)c->n)->istat);
+	USED(((volatile Ncr*)c->n)->sist0);
+	USED(((volatile Ncr*)c->n)->sist1);
+	USED(((volatile Ncr*)c->n)->dstat);
 	if(!(c->v->feature & Wide))
 		ntarget = 8;
 	else
